	eyebrow="[ Statistics, Illustrated ]"
	title="The Noise Problem"
	subtitle="Most of what makes your metric noisy has nothing to do with your treatment. What if you could just remove it?"
	summary="A/B tests often take longer than they need to, not because the real effect is small, but because the metric itself is noisy for reasons that have nothing to do with the treatment. This project runs a live simulated experiment twice, once on the raw metric, once adjusted with CUPED, a real, widely-used variance-reduction technique, to measure exactly how much faster and more reliably it finds a genuine effect, without inflating false positives."
